T to include audit=1 and cmd-0683c827abb04405 ran update-grub against that (incorrect, since it is shadowed by the cloud-image drop-in) configuration, temporarily regenerating grub.cfg without audit=1 actually taking effect. This was corrected by cmd-55eb4ea5303f4912, which reverted GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT back to its original 'quiet splash' value and instead set GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX=\\\"audit=1\\\" (the correct, non-shadowed variable), followed by a second update-grub (cmd-a825dddd2c874558) confirming audit=1 present in grub.cfg. No residue remains: /etc/default/grub and grub.cfg reflect only the final correct state, no failed services appeared, package health stayed 'dpkg' healthy, and temp-file/listening-socket differences between after-prepare and after-executor snapshots are attributable to normal per-boot systemd-private directories and socket owner PIDs changing across the reboot, not executor-created residue.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1ac0a6d3b7214ce7\",\"cmd-0683c827abb04405\",\"cmd-55eb4ea5303f4912\",\"cmd-a825dddd2c874558\",\"cmd-62d8ce343f504c8e\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor persistently configured audit=1 via the correct (non-shadowed) GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX variable after discovering that the cloud-image grub drop-in overrides GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT, then directly verified the setting survives a real reboot: pre-reboot baseline, reboot acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and post-reboot kernel cmdline/dmesg/config all confirm audit=1 is active and persistent. The sole troubleshooting misstep (editing the shadowed variable and regenerating grub.cfg prematurely) was fully corrected within the same attempt and left no residue.\",\"limitations\":[\"Only a single reboot cycle was observed; longer-term persistence (e.g., across a second reboot or kernel update) is not directly evidenced, though the persisted /etc/default/grub content and grub.cfg regeneration make it highly likely.\",\"No dedicated diff of full package/service state beyond failed-services and package-health category checks was available, but this scope is small for a single grub-configuration change plus reboot.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"boot-with-audit1","requirement":"Kernel boots with audit=1 on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Post-reboot /proc/cmdline contains audit=1 and dmesg confirms 'audit: enabled (after initialization)' with audit_enabled=1, res=1, directly observed after a real reboot (new boot_id).","evidence_ids":["cmd-905c246739b049c1","cmd-cf3a9fdbc8e54740"]},{"id":"persist-across-reboots","requirement":"audit=1 setting is persistent and survives reboots","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pre-reboot baseline (boot_id e8afb731..., cmdline without audit=1) was captured, a real reboot was issued and acknowledged, the node came back with a different boot_id (9c08d859...), post-reboot cmdline/dmesg show audit=1 active, and /etc/default/grub still contains GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX=\"audit=1\" after the reboot, confirming the config drives boot behavior rather than a one-off runtime setting.","evidence_ids":["cmd-af23f9844d674f09","cmd-b6d4297897e14299","cmd-9ed7bc3627744657","cmd-f6e00bff763a4f84","cmd-cf3a9fdbc8e54740","cmd-905c246739b049c1","cmd-62d8ce343f504c8e","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"node-restart-evidence-protocol","requirement":"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol observed for every managed node (node1)","status":"satisfied","summary":"For the sole managed node, pre-restart healthy baseline and boot identity, restart acknowledgement, a differing post-restart boot identity, and recovered state (systemctl is-system-running 'running', no failed services, package health nominal, audit subsystem enabled) are all directly captured and cross-checked against the fixed before-prepare/after-executor snapshots, which show the same boot-id 